WHAT THE CURRENT INSTRUCTION GRANT DOES NOT YET CONTAIN.

Status: architecture COVERAGE LEDGER, not an operation grant.
It is part of the work required before anyone can honestly
call this a full ARMv7-A T32 instruction set definition.
Current allowed operations: numbered 01 through 72, all and
only those in the closed initial grant in this directory.

Primary standard: ARM DDI 0406C.d, A6 T32 encoding and A8
instruction details, with architecture restrictions throughout.
Secondary source for convenient family location: Arm assembly
guide 100076_0200_00_en, section C2 (integer/control), section
C3 (Advanced SIMD), section C4 (floating point).
The SECONDARY 2019 GUIDE ALSO DESCRIBES LATER ARMv8 and M
instructions and A32-only forms. Merely naming a family
in that guide is NOT evidence that it belongs to ARMv7-A T32.

READING THE STATUS OF A FAMILY.

COVERED-SUBSET: at least one machine effect is named in the
present 72-operation grant, but encoding-by-encoding validation
and exhaustive forms remain unfinished.
UNGRANTED: the hardware may provide this instruction family,
but the English Major C profile does not yet name permitted
effects/encodings for it.
DECODED-BUT-UNGRANTED: the architectural encoding has been
identified (including any exception-generating behavior),
but the closed English Major C operation grant does not
authorize it as an executable source operation. A decoded
UDF or SVC is an example; these two have different
architectural effects and may NOT be conflated.
TARGET-DEPENDENT: extension or feature/privilege conditions
must be verified before any admission.
NOT-ARMV7-A-ASSUMED: later-architecture or another profile's
instruction cannot be imported merely from another reference.
Nothing in the status vocabulary says that the full architecture
has been exhaustively enumerated.

PART ONE. EVERYDAY EXECUTION MACHINERY.

COVERED-SUBSET: advance or decrease SP by a seven-bit immediate
scaled by FOUR bytes, using the dedicated 16-bit T32 ADD SP
and SUB SP forms. See operations 59 and 60. All broader
SP arithmetic forms and address-generation variants
remain ungranted until separately named and verified.

COVERED-SUBSET: 16-bit Thumb PUSH with a nonempty set
of R0..R7 and optional LR, and 16-bit Thumb POP with a
nonempty set of R0..R7 and optional PC. See operations
54 through 56 and the exact stack memory document.
Wide PUSH and POP aliases, single-register wide forms,
more general register sets and exceptional encodings
remain UNGRANTED and must not inherit permission.

COVERED-SUBSET: 16-bit T32 LDMIA and STMIA, with base
R0..R7, nonempty list in R0..R7, and the additional
language restriction that the base register is NOT
included among destinations or stored registers.
See operations 57 and 58. The permitted narrow forms
have writeback on successful completion. The legal
native base-overlap cases, most 32-bit Thumb forms,
STMDB, optional PC register-list entries, and other
address modes remain UNGRANTED by this subset.

COVERED-SUBSET: four additional FOUR-BYTE Thumb operations
(61 through 64), each assigning distinct semantics
to STMIA W=0, STMIA W=1, LDMDB W=0 and LDMDB W=1.
These admit selected low/high R0..R12 and optional LR
register lists of at least TWO members, but deliberately
exclude PC/SP and base-in-list overlap. The actual
encoding examples E884:4800, E8A4:4800,
E914:4800, and E934:4800 are preserved in
"Actual wide Thumb multiple-word operations in long
English.english" under the examples directory.
These are COVERED FOR THAT SUBSET ONLY; general wide
variants and independently accepted legal-encoding
coverage are still unfinished.

For PUSH/POP/LDM/STM, a successful trace does not
prove all-or-nothing behavior after a mid-transfer
memory fault. Precise permission, alignment,
architecture-specific exception and concurrency
details remain acceptance obligations.
Sources: DDI 0406C.d, guide C2.45/C2.73/C2.74/C2.138.

UNGRANTED: load/store a pair of words and account for
alignment, ordering, register pairs and writeback.
Sources: LDRD/STRD; architectural A8 instruction details.

COVERED-SUBSET: single scalar loads and stores and
PC-relative literal loads. Exact register offset,
pre-index, post-index, alignment, literal-pool data,
endianness, permissions and exceptions still require
encoding-specific validation. Sources: LDR, STR,
LDRB/H, LDRSB/SH, STRB/H and the A8 variants.

COVERED-SUBSET: direct and indirect branches, call/return,
conditional zero branches, IT, table branches. The
multiple encoding widths and full legal conditional
combinations are NOT yet covered. Sources: B, BL,
BX, BLX, CBZ, CBNZ, IT, TBB, TBH.

COVERED-SUBSET: the ENTIRE 4096-halfword 16-bit decode
space 0xD000..0xDFFF has now been individually enumerated,
using ARM DDI 0406C.d A6.2.6 Table A6-8:
  14 distinct T1 condition-code groups, 256 encodings
  each, totaling 3584 conditional branches;
  256 permanently undefined UDF T1 encodings;
  256 SVC T1 supervisor-call encodings.
Only the B T1 class is granted through operation 43,
and ONLY outside an IT block. DDI 0406C.d A8.8.18
expressly declares T1 B in IT UNPREDICTABLE.
See "Every conditional-branch and supervisor-call
halfword.english" and the two 4096-entry and
224-entry condition/flag TSV fixtures.

A THIRD completely enumerated sixteen-bit Thumb
decoding subtree is BF00..BFFF, 256 halfwords,
under "Every If Then and hint halfword.english":
  214 potentially legal IT T1 encodings, with
  additional surrounding ITSTATE/follower restrictions;
  26 firstcond/mask patterns UNPREDICTABLE under
  DDI 0406C.d A8.8.55 and REJECTED by the grant;
  one exact NOP BF00, permitted by operation 52;
  four architecturally named but UNGRANTED hints
  YIELD, WFE, WFI and SEV;
  eleven UNALLOCATED HINT patterns that execute as
  NOP-like hints but software MUST NOT use.
The 256 rows and permission distinctions are:
fixtures/armv7-a-t32-if-then-and-hint-halfwords.tsv
The full ITSTATE runtime interpreter and all legal
following-instruction cases are NOT yet verified.
Operation 46's permitted IT cases cannot excuse a
T1 conditional B inside IT.

The previously audited 0xBA00..0xBAFF subtree
has 64 REV, 64 REV16, 64 UNDEFINED,
and 64 REVSH halfwords. Combined with the
0xD000..0xDFFF subtree and the BF00..BFFF
subtree, the THREE COMPLETED DECODE SUBTREES
account for exactly 256+4096+256=4608 DISTINCT
NUMERICAL 16-bit halfword values, not an
exhaustive ARM Thumb instruction set.
Uninspected instruction spaces and 32-bit
instruction encodings remain open obligations.

DECODED-BUT-UNGRANTED: all 256 SVC T1 instruction encodings
0xDF00..0xDFFF are recognized under DDI 0406C.d A6.2.6,
Table A6-8. SVC is architecturally a supervisor exception
request that can be issued by unprivileged user-mode code;
it is not simply a forbidden privileged instruction.
The required exception context, state save, return path,
OS/ABI-specific service convention and effects are
not part of the current language grant. A decoder
recognizing DF01 must not claim a successful English Major C
system call occurred.

DECODED-BUT-UNGRANTED: all 256 UDF T1 encodings
0xDE00..0xDEFF belong to the permanently undefined
exception-generating class under Table A6-8.
This includes 0xDEF9 and 0xDEFE, whose diagnostic
LLVM 17 spellings are "__brkdiv0" and "trap".
Those foreign aliases do not make extra operations.
UDF differs from SVC and from normal instruction
fall-through. The current English Major C grant
contains no general exception-taking operation.
UNGRANTED: other deliberate breakpoint and debug
entry mechanisms (BKPT, DBG), whose exact effect
and environment still need separate specifications.

PART TWO. ARITHMETIC THAT REAL PROGRAMS MAY NEED.

COVERED-SUBSET: ADD, ADC, SUB, SBC, RSB, CMP, CMN,
logical operations, ordinary shifts, rotate, multiply
low 32 bits and the specific 16-bit MOVS-immediate.
Actual encodings and per-form flag effects remain open.

UNGRANTED: multiply-add and multiply-subtract using
separate accumulators, even when they can be built
from other arithmetic operations. Source: MLA/MLS,
guide C2.56-C2.57.

UNGRANTED: full 64-bit signed and unsigned multiply,
multiply-accumulate and long results, including
the behavior of high and low destination registers.
Sources: SMULL, UMULL, SMLAL, UMLAL and variants.

TARGET-DEPENDENT: signed and unsigned integer division.
SDIV/UDIV support is optional for an ARMv7-A processor
and may differ between the A32 and T32 instruction
sets. Actual feature-register evidence is required.
Source: guide C2.99 and C2.163.

UNGRANTED: more complicated signed halfword multiplies,
dual multiplies and signed absolute-difference
accumulations. Sources include SMLAxy, SMLAD,
SMLSD, SMLALD, SMUAD, SMUSD, USAD8, USADA8.
Distinct signedness, overflow and accumulation
rules must be preserved by each future long name.

UNGRANTED: arithmetic saturation, saturation flags,
and halfword/byte parallel integer arithmetic.
Sources include SSAT, USAT, QADD, QSUB,
QADD16/QSUB16, UADD8/UADD16 and related
families. These cannot be assumed equivalent to
ordinary modulo-2^32 addition.

PART THREE. REARRANGEMENT, PACKING AND FLAGS.

COVERED-SUBSET: operations 69 through 72 now grant one
specified four-byte T32 form apiece for BFC, BFI, UBFX
and SBFX with source and destination general registers
R0..R12 and nonempty contiguous bit intervals
that fit entirely within positions 0..31. They
distinguish zero-extension from sign-extension and
clear from insertion. General instruction aliases,
registers outside the declared set, unrelated forms
and any encoding not yet independently checked
remain ungranted.

COVERED-SUBSET: operations 65-67 grant the TWO-byte
T32 T1 REV, REV16 and REVSH forms with low-register
source/destination R0-R7. Operation 68 grants a
specified FOUR-byte RBIT form with general-register
operands R0-R12. REV and RBIT have different bit
permutations. REVSH sign-extends; it does not merely
zero-extend a byte-swapped halfword. These operations
do not change memory byte ordering or endian state.

A COMPLETE SMALL DECODE SUBTREE has separately been
accounted for: 0xBA00..0xBAFF includes 64 REV,
64 REV16, 64 unallocated/UNDEFINED halfwords and
64 REVSH, with all 256 individual data rows under
fixtures/armv7-a-t32-byte-reversal-halfwords.tsv.
See "Every halfword in one complete Thumb decode
region.english". That closed subtree is NOT
a complete audit of all T32 instructions.

UNGRANTED: rotate a 32-bit value through the incoming
carry flag, producing the appropriate resulting
carry. Source: RRX. This is not operation 25,
which rotates a complete 32-bit word without an
extra carry-in bit.

UNGRANTED: test bitwise exclusive-or for N/Z and
the specific shifter-carry effects without storing
a result. Source: TEQ. Operation 16 presently
covers AND-based TST, not the different TEQ predicate.

UNGRANTED: OR a source with the bitwise complement
of another source. Source: ORN (T32 form).
This may be constructible as a multi-instruction
sequence but is still a distinct hardware encoding.

UNGRANTED: packet/halfword packing, select bits
based on GE flags, and explicitly update
architectural GE flags where required.
Sources: PKHBT/PKHTB, SEL and packed
parallel arithmetic groups.

PART FOUR. SYNCHRONIZATION, MEMORY AND INTERRUPTS.

UNGRANTED: exclusive load/store reservation and
success/failure status, and explicit clearing
of an outstanding reservation. Sources: LDREX,
STREX, CLREX and any associated byte/halfword/
doubleword exclusive variants available to the
actual architectural target.

UNGRANTED: memory-ordering and instruction-
synchronization barriers whose architectural
effects differ. Sources: DMB, DSB, ISB.
They are not substitutes for one another and
cannot simply be omitted by a translator.

UNGRANTED: prefetch instruction/data/cache hints.
Sources: PLD, PLDW, PLI and hints. Their effects
cannot be assumed to equal an architecturally
successful data load or executable branch.

UNGRANTED: wait-for-interrupt/event, send-event
and yield hints. Sources: WFI, WFE, SEV, YIELD.
Their instruction permissions and observable
effects require target-context interpretation.

UNGRANTED: change processor mode, interrupt masks
or data endianness, and exception returns.
Sources include CPS, SETEND, RFE, SRS,
and SUBS PC, LR where legal.
The unprivileged English Major C profile
does not grant supervisor authority just
because a mnemonic exists in the manual.

TARGET-DEPENDENT: low-level coprocessor,
architectural identification, debug, virtualization
and trust-zone operations. Sources include
MCR/MRC, MCRR/MRRC, CDP, MRS/MSR,
SMC/HVC and BXJ. These require separate
architecture and permission rules; they cannot
be described as plain user-space function calls.

PART FIVE. FLOATING POINT AND VECTOR EXTENSIONS.

TARGET-DEPENDENT: VFP/Advanced SIMD register files,
instruction presence, save/restore, rounding,
denormals, NaNs, exceptions and status registers.
A physical target must declare whether these
facilities exist and are made available to
the current operating process.

UNGRANTED: move values between scalar general-purpose
registers and extension registers. Floating-point
load/store and arithmetic. Floating-point compare
and transfer of comparison state to condition flags.
Sources include VMOV, VLDR, VSTR, VADD, VSUB,
VMUL, VDIV, VCMP, VMRS, VMSR and the applicable
VFP instruction encodings, with extension restrictions.

UNGRANTED: Advanced SIMD data layout and arithmetic,
multi-register loads/stores, lane extraction and
insertion, saturation, narrowing, widening and
horizontal reduction. The full instruction families
in the Arm reference require an independent
coverage inventory rather than a single generic
"perform SIMD" instruction.

NOT-ARMV7-A-ASSUMED: ARMv8-only cryptography,
CRC, acquire/release/other later forms, Armv8-M
TrustZone and other later/profile-specific
instructions. The 2019 Arm instruction guide
includes some of these and therefore its table
of contents is NOT a proof of support by
ARMv7-A processors.

PART SIX. THE REQUIRED NEXT INVENTORY METHOD.

Start with the actual ARM DDI 0406C.d A6 decoding
tables, NOT merely a list of popular mnemonics.
For every valid T32 encoding under the chosen
architectural feature profile, record:

  * the exact first/second halfword discriminator;
  * the official encoding identifier and any alias;
  * whether the architecture feature is mandatory;
  * which registers, immediates and IT contexts
    are permitted and which are unpredictable;
  * the effect on general registers, flags,
    PC-operand reads, execution index, ITSTATE,
    memory, exception state and special registers;
  * the exact long English name and its versioned
    permitted-operation identity, OR an explicit
    exclusion and reason;
  * independent correct and incorrect test inputs;
  * source/encode/decode/semantic/hardware evidence
    at each of those separate assurance levels.

Only AFTER the decoding table audit covers every
leaf and every architectural exception may the
complete T32 hardware-catalogue claim be made.
The present file does not perform that audit.
It does prevent the unfinished work from being
silently forgotten or mislabeled complete.

TRACKING.
